<div class="poster">

  <!-- HEADER -->
  <div class="header">
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Global Economy in the Shadow of War:<br>IMF World Economic Outlook Trends 2024–2026</h1>
      <div class="subtitle">A synthesis of IMF World Economic Outlook projections from 2024 through 2026, tracking the trajectory of global growth, inflation, and evolving policy priorities amid rising geopolitical uncertainty.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="header-right">
      <div class="venue">IMF–World Bank<br>Annual Meetings<br>Research Symposium<br>2026</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY STATS BANNER -->
  <div class="stat-banner">
    <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3.0%</div><div class="stat-label">Global Growth 2025 (WEO Jul 2025)</div></div>
    <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3.3%</div><div class="stat-label">Global Growth 2026 (WEO Jan 2026)</div></div>
    <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3.2%</div><div class="stat-label">Global Growth 2027 (WEO Jan 2026)</div></div>
    <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">8</div><div class="stat-label">WEO Editions Reviewed</div></div>
    <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value risk">⬇ Tilted</div><div class="stat-label">Risk Balance Across All Editions</div></div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 1: GROWTH TRAJECTORY -->
  <div class="section-growth">
    <div class="section-title">Global Growth Projections: A Narrow Band of Mediocrity</div>
    <div class="section-desc">IMF global growth forecasts have hovered between 3.0% and 3.3% across eight WEO editions — well below the pre-pandemic historical average of ~3.8%. The pattern shows persistent underperformance without collapse.</div>
    <div class="chart-container" id="growth-chart"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 2: DOWNSIDE RISKS -->
  <div class="section-risks">
    <div class="section-title">
      Dominant Downside Risks
      <span class="risk-warning">Tilted to the downside in every edition</span>
    </div>
    <div class="risk-list">
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon">1</div>
        <div class="risk-text">
          <h4>Trade Policy Escalation</h4>
          <p>Tariff increases and retaliatory measures from 2025 onward. Front-loading of activity ahead of expected tariffs temporarily masks structural damage.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon">2</div>
        <div class="risk-text">
          <h4>Geopolitical Tensions &amp; Conflict</h4>
          <p>Middle East instability, broader security concerns, and the war referenced in the April 2026 WEO title create persistent uncertainty.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon">3</div>
        <div class="risk-text">
          <h4>Technology Re-evaluation Risk</h4>
          <p>Elevated expectations around AI and tech investment face potential correction, threatening growth premia and capital flows.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon">4</div>
        <div class="risk-text">
          <h4>Persistent Services Inflation</h4>
          <p>Particularly in the United States, where services inflation is projected to return to target more gradually than in peer advanced economies.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 3: INFLATION -->
  <div class="section-inflation">
    <div class="section-title">Inflation Outlook and Monetary Policy</div>
    <div class="section-desc">Global disinflation enables policy pivot, but the path is uneven across economies.</div>
    <div class="inflation-points">
      <div class="inflation-point">
        <div class="dot down"></div>
        <span>Global headline inflation continues declining through 2026</span>
      </div>
      <div class="inflation-point">
        <div class="dot down"></div>
        <span>Central banks began easing — the "Policy Pivot" (WEO Oct 2024)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="inflation-point">
        <div class="dot warn"></div>
        <span>Services inflation particularly sticky, slowing disinflation</span>
      </div>
      <div class="inflation-point">
        <div class="dot warn"></div>
        <span>Renewed inflationary pressures by April 2026 — defense spending &amp; supply disruptions</span>
      </div>
      <div class="inflation-point">
        <div class="dot neutral"></div>
        <span>US inflation projected to remain above target longer than peers</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 4: US EXCEPTION -->
  <div class="section-us">
    <div class="section-title">The US Exception: Slower Return to Target</div>
    <div class="us-callout">
      <div class="big-text">US inflation lags peer economies</div>
      <div class="detail">While global headline inflation is expected to continue falling, US services-driven inflation is projected to return to target more gradually than in other advanced economies. This divergence complicates the global monetary easing cycle and keeps the Federal Reserve on a different trajectory from the ECB and other major central banks.</div>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-top:12px; font-size:12px; color: var(--text-secondary); padding: 10px 14px; background: #f8f9fb; border-radius: 4px; line-height: 1.5;">
      <strong>Key implication:</strong> The uneven disinflation path means the "policy pivot" is asynchronous — some central banks cut while others hold, creating exchange rate and capital flow pressures for emerging markets.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 5: POLICY EVOLUTION -->
  <div class="section-policy">
    <div class="section-title">Evolving Policy Priorities: From Fiscal Buffers to Defense-Spending Trade-offs</div>
    <div class="section-desc">IMF policy guidance has shifted meaningfully across six phases, reflecting changing risks and economic conditions.</div>
    <div class="policy-timeline">
      <div class="policy-step">
        <div class="node"></div>
        <div class="period">Mid-2024</div>
        <div class="focus">Manage disinflation;<br>begin monetary<br>easing carefully</div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-step">
        <div class="node"></div>
        <div class="period">Late 2024</div>
        <div class="focus">Pivot monetary<br>policy; ensure<br>fiscal sustainability</div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-step">
        <div class="node"></div>
        <div class="period">Early 2025</div>
        <div class="focus">Rebuild fiscal<br>buffers; address<br>demographic gaps</div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-step">
        <div class="node"></div>
        <div class="period">Mid-2025</div>
        <div class="focus">Restore confidence<br>amid tariff<br>uncertainty</div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-step">
        <div class="node"></div>
        <div class="period">Late 2025</div>
        <div class="focus">Strengthen policy<br>frameworks; cautious<br>industrial policy</div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-step">
        <div class="node"></div>
        <div class="period">Early 2026</div>
        <div class="focus">Agile policy;<br>defense-spending<br>trade-offs</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 6: THEMATIC FOCUS -->
  <div class="section-themes">
    <div class="section-title">Special Analytical Themes Across WEO Editions</div>
    <div class="themes-grid">
      <div class="theme-card">
        <div class="icon">🏭</div>
        <h5>Industrial Policy</h5>
